inistration\n\nRe: BRINEURA  \nPatent No.: 8,029,781  \nDocket No.: FDA-2017-E-6527\n\nThe Honorable Andrei Iancu  \nUnder Secretary of Commerce for Intellectual Property  \nDirector, United States Patent and Trademark Office  \nMail Stop Hatch-Waxman PTE  \nP.O. Box 1450  \nAlexandria, VA 22313-1450\n\nOCT 15 2018\n\nDear Director Iancu:\n\nThis is in regard to the application for patent term extension for U.S. Patent No. 8,029,781, filed by Rutgers, the State University of New Jersey, under 35 U.S.C. section 156 et seq. We have reviewed the dates contained in the application and have determined the regulatory review period for BRINEURA (cerliponase alfa), the human biological product claimed by the patent.\n\nThe total length of the regulatory review period for BRINEURA is 995 days. Of this time, 659 days occurred during the testing phase and 336 days occurred during the approval phase. These periods of time were derived from the following dates:\n\n1. The date an exemption under subsection 505(i) of the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act involving this biologic product became effective: August 8, 2014.\n\n   FDA has verified the Rutgers, the State University of New Jersey’s claim that the date the investigational new drug application became effective was on August 8, 2014.\n\n2. The date the application was initially submitted with respect to the biologics license application under section 351 of the Public Health Service Act: May 27, 2016.\n\n   FDA has verified the applicant's claim that the biologics license application (BLA) for BRINEURA (BLA 761052) was initially submitted on May 27, 2016.\n\n3. The date the application was approved: April 27, 2017.\n\n   FDA has verified the applicant's claim that BLA 761052 was approved on April 27, 2017.\n\nThis determination of the regulatory review period by FDA does not take into account the effective date of the patent, nor does it exclude one-half of the testing phase as required by 35 U.S.C. section 156(c)(2).\n\n